/*!

\page css-styling-applications.html
\title Styling Applications with CSS

\section1 Creating and Managing Styles

To create a new style for Union's CSS input, you can use \c{union-styletool}.

\c{union-styletool create} will interactively create an empty style, with
provided metadata. The style will be placed into your home folder by default,
but this can be customised with the \c{--output-path <output-path>} argument.

The created style will have the following structure:

\list
    \li contents/
    \list
        \li css/
        \li images/
    \endlist
    \li metadata.json
\endlist

\c{metadata.json} contains the metadata provided when creating the style.

In the \c{css/} folder, you should place the style's CSS, and in \c{images/},
any image assets for the style.

To find out more about how to create CSS for a Union style, please refer to
the
\l{built-in Breeze style}{https://invent.kde.org/plasma/union/-/tree/master/src/input/css/styles/breeze}
as an example, and to \l{css-support.html}{Supported CSS}, the
\l{css-properties.html}{CSS Property Reference}, and the
\l{css-functions.html}{CSS Function Reference}.

After installing your style with \c{union-styletool install <path>}, you can now
select it in the \i{Application Style} settings page.

\c{union-styletool} has other commands important for managing Union styles:

/*!
    \list
        \li \c{create} - Create a new style
        \li \c{inspect} - Inspect details of an installed style
        \li \c{install} - Install a new style
        \li \c{list} - List installed styles
        \li \c{update} - Update an installed style
        \li \c{uninstall} - Uninstall an installed style
        \li \c{package} - Package a style to a single shareable file
    \endlist
*/

\section1 What You Can and Cannot Style

Union strives to provide a generic "middle layer" that styles can provide styling
information for and that outputs can consume to apply the styling. This middle
layer includes the definition of what elements there are, what properties these
elements have that you can use for styling and what kind of sub-elements there
are. See the \l{elements.html}{Element Reference} for details.

While Union's goal is to give as much freedom to you as style creator to style
things the way you want, there are inherent limitations in what Union can do due
to how the underlying technology works.

The most significant limitation is that the application, and not Union, is the one
that decides the layout of things. This means that, unless otherwise noted, you
will not be able to control the position of elements. For sizes, the application
is also in final control, but you \i{can} provide an initial size for most elements.

The exception to this is that there are certain elements that consists of several
sub-elements. Union is generally in control of the placement of these
sub-elements, which allows you as style creator to influence them.


*/
